{"parentUuid":"7c803e1c-01b4-4baa-af26-ec3777e8f1cf","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01UEvx6zFMLbeWB37GUkrW8Y","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"if [ -n \"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_SPOOL_DIR:-}\" ] && [ -x /bin/zsh ]; then exec /bin/zsh -fc 'LC_ALL=C␤export CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-$PPID}␤export CMUX_AGENT_HOOK_INVOKER_PID=$PPID␤cmux_fallback() { { print -rn -- \"$cmux_p\"; /bin/cat; } | /bin/sh -c \"$1\" >&3; exit $?; }␤zmodload zsh/system zsh/datetime zsh/files 2>/dev/null || exec /bin/sh -c \"$1\" >&3␤cmux_p= cmux_c= cmux_e=0␤while (( ${#cmux_p} < 262144 )); do sysread -i 0 -s 65536 cmux_c || { cmux_e=$?; break; }; cmux_p+=$cmux_c; done␤[[ -n ${CMUX_SURFACE_ID:-} && ${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED:-} != 1 ]] || { (( cmux_e == 5 )) || /bin/cat >/dev/null; print -r -- '\\''{}'\\'' >&3; exit 0; }␤cmux_d=$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_SPOOL_DIR␤(( cmux_e == 5 )) && [[ -f $cmux_d/keys ]] || cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤cmux_r=\"cmux-agent-hook-v1\"$'\\''\\n'\\''\"claude\"$'\\''\\n'\\''\"$2\"$'\\''\\n'\\''␤for cmux_k in ${(f)\"$(<$cmux_d/keys)\"}; do (( ${+parameters[$cmux_k]} )) && cmux_r+=\"$cmux_k=${(P)cmux_k}\"$'\\''\\0'\\''; done␤cmux_r+=$'\\''\\0'\\''\"$cmux_p\"␤(( ${#cmux_r} <= 524288 )) || cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤umask 077␤cmux_n=$cmux_d/$epochtime[1].$epochtime[2]-$$␤{ print -rn -- \"$cmux_r\" >| $cmux_n.tmp && mv -f -- $cmux_n.tmp $cmux_n.rec; } 2>/dev/null || { rm -f -- $cmux_n.tmp 2>/dev/null; cmux_fallback \"$1\"; }␤if zsystem flock -t 0 -r -f cmux_l $cmux_d/forwarder.lock 2>/dev/null; then␤  zsystem flock -u $cmux_l␤  rm -- $cmux_n.rec 2>/dev/null && cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤fi␤print -r -- '\\''{}'\\'' >&3' cmux-hook 'cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; fi' pre-tool-use 3>&1 1>&2; else cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; fi; fi","durationMs":36},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6e03fad3-4d61-4a55-a11d-efb9cafbab59","timestamp":"2026-10-31T15:52:49.758Z","session_id":"135a2369-1f42-433b-aacc-5c1b0ce18b48","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"135a2369-1f42-433b-aacc-5c1b0ce18b48","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"pure-pondering-goose"}
{"parentUuid":"7c803e1c-01b4-4baa-af26-ec3777e8f1cf","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2584e434-b1b2-49e4-ba05-8073c4b5dacb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UEvx6zFMLbeWB37GUkrW8Y","type":"tool_result","content":"### 3.1 Hooks, properly\n\n- Dispatch by event **name**, not three hardcoded methods. Add `SessionStart`,\n  `SessionEnd`, `PreCompact`, `SubagentStop`, `UserPromptSubmit`.\n- Support the **JSON stdout protocol** alongside the exit-code protocol:\n  `permissionDecision`, `additionalContext`, and the reason field. Exit code 2\n  keeps working; a hook that returns JSON is no longer ignored.\n- Keep the failure semantics the module already has and that\n  `name-the-swallow` is about: a hook that errors is **recorded and stood aside\n  from**, never fatal.\n\n### 3.2 `permissions` from settings.json\n\n`deny` subtracts from the tool grant and records a rung-1 event. `allow` is\nalready the default here and is a no-op. `ask` is recorded and treated as\n`deny` — with a line in the progress log saying so, because the alternative is\nan unattended factory quietly auto-approving what a repository asked a human\nabout.\n\n### 3.3 `@path` imports in `CLAUDE.md`\n\nClaude Code expands `@relative/path` in `CLAUDE.md` into the referenced file's\ncontents. A repo that splits its charter this way is currently **half-read** by\n`custom` — the worst possible outcome, since the missing half looks like a\ncharter that simply did not say much.\n\nResolve recursively, depth-capped, cycle-detected, confined to the repo. Record\nevery file pulled in, so the charter's token cost is the real cost.\n\n### 3.4 `.claude/agents/*.md` — sub-agent types\n\n`task` takes an instruction and an optional tool subset. Claude Code lets a repo\ndefine named agents with their own prompt, tool list and model. The mesh routing\nin `subagent_handler` already carries everything needed; what is missing is\nreading the definitions and passing a name.\n\nThe one invariant that must not bend: a child's grant stays a **strict subset**\nof its parent's, whatever the file says. `child_grant` already enforces this by\nconstruction and should keep doing so — an agent definition that asks for a tool\nits caller does not hold gets the intersection, and the shortfall is recorded.\nOtherwise a repo could define its way around rung 1, which is a hole in the\nladder shaped exactly like a feature.\n\n---\n\n### 3b, as built: the charter surfaces a repo already ships\n\nAll three, and the common thread is that each was a surface a repository had\nalready written and this harness was reading past.\n\n**Hooks dispatch by event name.** Three events were hardcoded as methods —\n`before`, `after`, `stopping` — and the other five Claude Code defines were\nunreachable. `fire(event, tool, **fields)` replaces all three, and\n`SessionStart`, `SessionEnd`, `PreCompact`, `SubagentStop` and\n`UserPromptSubmit` now happen:\n\n- `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` before the first model call. What they\n  add goes into the transcript; a `UserPromptSubmit` refusal **fails the turn**\n  rather than being noted, because a brief this repository will not accept is not\nfollow cheaply once the dispatcher takes an event name rather than three\nhardcoded ones.\n\n**Hook JSON protocol.** Claude Code's hooks may return JSON on stdout —\n`permissionDecision`, `additionalContext`, and so on — rather than only\nsignalling through exit codes. `hooks.py` reads exit code 2 and stderr. A repo\nwhose hook returns JSON gets its decision ignored under `custom`. That is the\nsame class of silence rung 2 was built to remove.\n\n**`permissions` in settings.json.** `allow` / `deny` / `ask` lists are a\nrepository declaring rung 1 in a file, next to the hooks that already work.\n`deny` maps cleanly onto subtracting from the tool grant. `ask` has no meaning\nin an unattended factory and should be recorded and treated as `deny`, loudly,\nrather than quietly as `allow`.\n\n**MCP, deferred with a trigger rather than a shrug.** The existing plan defers\n**Answer:** the factory reads the charter *out of the sprite* and passes it as\n`append_system_prompt`. That is one `sprite::code::read` and a payload field, and\nit makes rung 0 explicit rather than ambient.\n\n### Rung 2 — the repository's own hooks do not fire\n\nTwice over. The repo's `.claude/settings.json` is not loaded (wrong directory),\nand its matchers are written against `Bash`, `Write` and `Edit` — which no turn\nhere calls any more. A matcher for `Bash(php *)` will never see\n`mcp__wipp__sprite__exec`.\n\n**Answer, and it needs a decision:** either translate the repository's matchers\nonto the sprite tool names before the turn, or run its hooks *in the sprite*\naround the command. The first is a small amount of Python and is honest about\nbeing a translation; the second is closer to what the repository meant. This is\nthe largest open design question in the migration.\n\n### `eval` reads `harness::metrics`\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"fd483259-0cfa-4258-86ac-9da3de57b246","timestamp":"2026-10-31T15:52:50.866Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"### 3.1 Hooks, properly\n\n- Dispatch by event **name**, not three hardcoded methods. Add `SessionStart`,\n  `SessionEnd`, `PreCompact`, `SubagentStop`, `UserPromptSubmit`.\n- Support the **JSON stdout protocol** alongside the exit-code protocol:\n  `permissionDecision`, `additionalContext`, and the reason field. Exit code 2\n  keeps working; a hook that returns JSON is no longer ignored.\n- Keep the failure semantics the module already has and that\n  `name-the-swallow` is about: a hook that errors is **recorded and stood aside\n  from**, never fatal.\n\n### 3.2 `permissions` from settings.json\n\n`deny` subtracts from the tool grant and records a rung-1 event. `allow` is\nalready the default here and is a no-op. `ask` is recorded and treated as\n`deny` — with a line in the progress log saying so, because the alternative is\nan unattended factory quietly auto-approving what a repository asked a human\nabout.\n\n### 3.3 `@path` imports in `CLAUDE.md`\n\nClaude Code expands `@relative/path` in `CLAUDE.md` into the referenced file's\ncontents. A repo that splits its charter this way is currently **half-read** by\n`custom` — the worst possible outcome, since the missing half looks like a\ncharter that simply did not say much.\n\nResolve recursively, depth-capped, cycle-detected, confined to the repo. Record\nevery file pulled in, so the charter's token cost is the real cost.\n\n### 3.4 `.claude/agents/*.md` — sub-agent types\n\n`task` takes an instruction and an optional tool subset. Claude Code lets a repo\ndefine named agents with their own prompt, tool list and model. The mesh routing\nin `subagent_handler` already carries everything needed; what is missing is\nreading the definitions and passing a name.\n\nThe one invariant that must not bend: a child's grant stays a **strict subset**\nof its parent's, whatever the file says. `child_grant` already enforces this by\nconstruction and should keep doing so — an agent definition that asks for a tool\nits caller does not hold gets the intersection, and the shortfall is recorded.\nOtherwise a repo could define its way around rung 1, which is a hole in the\nladder shaped exactly like a feature.\n\n---\n\n### 3b, as built: the charter surfaces a repo already ships\n\nAll three, and the common thread is that each was a surface a repository had\nalready written and this harness was reading past.\n\n**Hooks dispatch by event name.** Three events were hardcoded as methods —\n`before`, `after`, `stopping` — and the other five Claude Code defines were\nunreachable. `fire(event, tool, **fields)` replaces all three, and\n`SessionStart`, `SessionEnd`, `PreCompact`, `SubagentStop` and\n`UserPromptSubmit` now happen:\n\n- `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` before the first model call. What they\n  add goes into the transcript; a `UserPromptSubmit` refusal **fails the turn**\n  rather than being noted, because a brief this repository will not accept is not\nfollow cheaply once the dispatcher takes an event name rather than three\nhardcoded ones.\n\n**Hook JSON protocol.** Claude Code's hooks may return JSON on stdout —\n`permissionDecision`, `additionalContext`, and so on — rather than only\nsignalling through exit codes. `hooks.py` reads exit code 2 and stderr. A repo\nwhose hook returns JSON gets its decision ignored under `custom`. That is the\nsame class of silence rung 2 was built to remove.\n\n**`permissions` in settings.json.** `allow` / `deny` / `ask` lists are a\nrepository declaring rung 1 in a file, next to the hooks that already work.\n`deny` maps cleanly onto subtracting from the tool grant. `ask` has no meaning\nin an unattended factory and should be recorded and treated as `deny`, loudly,\nrather than quietly as `allow`.\n\n**MCP, deferred with a trigger rather than a shrug.** The existing plan defers\n**Answer:** the factory reads the charter *out of the sprite* and passes it as\n`append_system_prompt`. That is one `sprite::code::read` and a payload field, and\nit makes rung 0 explicit rather than ambient.\n\n### Rung 2 — the repository's own hooks do not fire\n\nTwice over. The repo's `.claude/settings.json` is not loaded (wrong directory),\nand its matchers are written against `Bash`, `Write` and `Edit` — which no turn\nhere calls any more. A matcher for `Bash(php *)` will never see\n`mcp__wipp__sprite__exec`.\n\n**Answer, and it needs a decision:** either translate the repository's matchers\nonto the sprite tool names before the turn, or run its hooks *in the sprite*\naround the command. The first is a small amount of Python and is honest about\nbeing a translation; the second is closer to what the repository meant. This is\nthe largest open design question in the migration.\n\n### `eval` reads `harness::metrics`","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/tacoda/tac